WEBVTT

00:00.210 --> 00:01.230
Instructor: All right so imagine,

00:01.230 --> 00:02.760
we have this hypothetical repo

00:02.760 --> 00:05.430
with three commits that we cloned

00:05.430 --> 00:06.960
and there's a master branch,

00:06.960 --> 00:07.793
and them, of course,

00:07.793 --> 00:10.560
we have this remote tracking branch we talked about

00:10.560 --> 00:13.260
that says this is the last known spot that we know of

00:13.260 --> 00:14.880
for origin slash master,

00:14.880 --> 00:18.120
the master branch on the origin GitHub repo.

00:18.120 --> 00:19.770
Anyway, I do some more work.

00:19.770 --> 00:22.650
What happens if I'm on the master branch I make new commits?

00:22.650 --> 00:24.390
That master pointer updates

00:24.390 --> 00:26.850
just like it always has when we've made branches

00:26.850 --> 00:28.890
and worked on branches and made new commits.

00:28.890 --> 00:30.900
That branch reference moves,

00:30.900 --> 00:33.870
it refers to the latest commit on that branch.

00:33.870 --> 00:36.330
So I might have two new commits

00:36.330 --> 00:39.360
and you'll see the remote branch reference doesn't update,

00:39.360 --> 00:41.340
it doesn't move, that's the point.

00:41.340 --> 00:45.300
It stays at the last known spot for origin/master.

00:45.300 --> 00:47.250
This is just master.

00:47.250 --> 00:50.370
Origin/master is the master branch on origin,

00:50.370 --> 00:52.170
whatever that GitHub repo is.

00:52.170 --> 00:53.250
So let's demonstrate this.

00:53.250 --> 00:56.430
Let's make on this animals project, right?

00:56.430 --> 00:58.410
I've got my main branch,

00:58.410 --> 01:01.170
and again, instead of master, it's main this time.

01:01.170 --> 01:02.700
On this main branch,

01:02.700 --> 01:04.620
I'll go ahead and make some changes.

01:04.620 --> 01:05.910
I'll make a new commit.

01:05.910 --> 01:07.740
So we've got this pets file.

01:07.740 --> 01:08.580
Here it is.

01:08.580 --> 01:10.380
I'll make a change or two.

01:10.380 --> 01:13.530
Why don't I add my two childhood cats

01:13.530 --> 01:15.120
that are no longer with us.

01:15.120 --> 01:19.320
Let's see, we had Monty and Mocha,

01:19.320 --> 01:20.700
and then I'll add and commit.

01:20.700 --> 01:24.310
So git add pets, git commit -m

01:25.207 --> 01:28.800
"add childhood cats,"

01:28.800 --> 01:31.980
all right, so now we have two commits just locally

01:31.980 --> 01:35.580
on the main branch and if I type git status,

01:35.580 --> 01:37.740
we'll see something we haven't really looked at

01:37.740 --> 01:39.210
or talked about before.

01:39.210 --> 01:41.850
This right here, your branch is ahead

01:41.850 --> 01:45.420
of origin slash main by one commit.

01:45.420 --> 01:49.050
In other words, your main branch and origin/main

01:49.050 --> 01:51.990
were pointing at the exact same thing

01:51.990 --> 01:53.700
just like this except master.

01:53.700 --> 01:57.150
But anyway, your main branch and origin/main were pointing

01:57.150 --> 01:59.850
to the same commit but then you made a new commit,

01:59.850 --> 02:02.670
and so now you are one commit ahead

02:02.670 --> 02:05.010
of where origin/main left off.

02:05.010 --> 02:06.660
That's exactly what it's telling me.

02:06.660 --> 02:09.330
So if I do this again, I make another change.

02:09.330 --> 02:13.653
We had a tree frog as a kid named George the Frog,

02:15.630 --> 02:16.463
what was he?

02:16.463 --> 02:19.260
A red-eyed tree frog I think is what they were called.

02:19.260 --> 02:22.983
Really cool looking but not very friendly pets.

02:24.180 --> 02:27.603
Anyway, George the Frog, I'm gonna add him.

02:28.860 --> 02:31.687
git add pets, git commit

02:31.687 --> 02:35.970
"add George the frog."

02:35.970 --> 02:39.270
All right, so now I type git status once again.

02:39.270 --> 02:42.300
Now we see your main branch, which is what I'm on,

02:42.300 --> 02:45.540
right on branch main is ahead of origin slash main

02:45.540 --> 02:48.270
by two commits now, so in other words,

02:48.270 --> 02:52.410
we're at this point where there are now two commits

02:52.410 --> 02:55.200
that master has since the last known spot

02:55.200 --> 02:58.560
of origin/master or in our case origin/main.

02:58.560 --> 03:01.380
Anyway, they have diverged and that's fine

03:01.380 --> 03:02.640
that's totally normal, right?

03:02.640 --> 03:05.040
That's the point of this tracking reference.

03:05.040 --> 03:08.550
This remote tracking reference is so that you know

03:08.550 --> 03:09.900
how far ahead you are

03:09.900 --> 03:12.510
and it will update when we get new updates

03:12.510 --> 03:14.190
from the GitHub repo, right?

03:14.190 --> 03:15.450
We can change this,

03:15.450 --> 03:18.090
we can go ask to make sure are there new commits,

03:18.090 --> 03:19.140
am I missing stuff?

03:19.140 --> 03:21.210
And this might move somewhere,

03:21.210 --> 03:22.980
but anyway, we haven't gotten to that yet.

03:22.980 --> 03:24.990
That's where fetching and pulling come in.

03:24.990 --> 03:27.930
But what I wanna show you is that if you wanted to

03:27.930 --> 03:30.270
if you were ahead of origin/master

03:30.270 --> 03:32.610
or origin/main or whatever it is, like we are,

03:32.610 --> 03:36.270
we can still take a look at origin/master or origin/main.

03:36.270 --> 03:37.470
We can check it out.

03:37.470 --> 03:42.060
So imagine this is our scenario and we we wonder, hmm,

03:42.060 --> 03:43.290
what did this project look like

03:43.290 --> 03:44.940
when I first cloned this repository,

03:44.940 --> 03:48.270
or when I last got an update from origin.

03:48.270 --> 03:51.900
If you want to know, you can check out origin/master.

03:51.900 --> 03:53.850
So you will go into detached HEAD,

03:53.850 --> 03:55.410
just like anytime we've done this

03:55.410 --> 03:57.270
before checking out a commits,

03:57.270 --> 03:58.920
don't panic, don't freak out.

03:58.920 --> 04:00.120
So let me show this.

04:00.120 --> 04:01.590
I'm gonna go back here.

04:01.590 --> 04:04.440
We are ahead of origin/main by two commits.

04:04.440 --> 04:06.480
This is what it looks like right now

04:06.480 --> 04:08.070
when we have these two new commits, right?

04:08.070 --> 04:09.270
These are new lines.

04:09.270 --> 04:10.800
I type git log,

04:10.800 --> 04:12.450
three total commits.

04:12.450 --> 04:15.210
So if I go back, git checkout,

04:15.210 --> 04:17.310
and remember I can always see

04:17.310 --> 04:20.760
that remote tracking branch right there, origin/main.

04:20.760 --> 04:23.553
git checkout origin/main,

04:24.600 --> 04:26.490
okay, we're in detached HEAD,

04:26.490 --> 04:28.620
you can look around, blah, blah, blah.

04:28.620 --> 04:29.640
Here we are.

04:29.640 --> 04:30.900
This is what it looked like.

04:30.900 --> 04:34.500
This is that reference that bookmark to origin/main

04:34.500 --> 04:37.200
from the last time we communicated with GitHub,

04:37.200 --> 04:39.930
this is what we had for the main branch.

04:39.930 --> 04:42.390
So I've moved ahead by two commits.

04:42.390 --> 04:45.510
If I wanted to work from here, just like any time

04:45.510 --> 04:47.100
that we've been in detached HEAD,

04:47.100 --> 04:49.350
I can make a new branch and start working,

04:49.350 --> 04:51.960
or I can undo, I go back to main,

04:51.960 --> 04:53.400
if I just want to, you know, I'm done.

04:53.400 --> 04:55.650
I just wanted to poke around and see what it looks like.

04:55.650 --> 04:57.930
But this is just to show you really as an illustration

04:57.930 --> 05:01.530
of the fact that this is a reference to where we were,

05:01.530 --> 05:04.020
where the main branch was last time we communicated

05:04.020 --> 05:06.090
with GitHub and now I can go back just

05:06.090 --> 05:09.330
to the actual main branch, get switch main,

05:09.330 --> 05:11.520
and this is where we are now, right?

05:11.520 --> 05:13.230
On my local machine.

05:13.230 --> 05:15.240
So now of course if I want these two commits

05:15.240 --> 05:17.670
that I have that aren't on GitHub,

05:17.670 --> 05:19.170
if I want them up on GitHub,

05:19.170 --> 05:21.240
it's as simple as pushing them, right?

05:21.240 --> 05:24.390
git push origin master.

05:24.390 --> 05:26.070
Nope, origin main,

05:26.070 --> 05:27.180
old habits die hard.

05:27.180 --> 05:28.893
There we go, git push origin main,

05:30.270 --> 05:31.143
refresh,

05:32.370 --> 05:35.250
and we see we now have our new commit here,

05:35.250 --> 05:37.080
add George the Frog,

05:37.080 --> 05:39.540
I can take a look at pets.txt.

05:39.540 --> 05:42.240
Okay, so we have Monty, Mocha, and George the Frog,

05:42.240 --> 05:45.210
and if I look at all the commits on this repository,

05:45.210 --> 05:49.140
just go back, three total commits now.

05:49.140 --> 05:51.660
The original one and then two that we just pushed.

05:51.660 --> 05:53.370
And now if I type git status,

05:53.370 --> 05:55.530
we're no longer two commits ahead.

05:55.530 --> 05:58.650
It says your branch is up to date with origin/main.

05:58.650 --> 06:01.350
We just communicated with that GitHub repository

06:01.350 --> 06:03.330
that remote repo called origin,

06:03.330 --> 06:06.870
and now our local repo knows what origin/main looks like.

06:06.870 --> 06:09.330
It has a new version of that pointer, right?

06:09.330 --> 06:12.690
It's pointing at the same commit that main is pointing at.

06:12.690 --> 06:14.340
But if I make new commits on main

06:14.340 --> 06:15.870
then of course they diverge.

06:15.870 --> 06:18.210
So I'll do that one more time.

06:18.210 --> 06:19.350
I'm not gonna show you anything new,

06:19.350 --> 06:20.910
I'm just gonna repeat the process.

06:20.910 --> 06:23.310
Let's see, another pet that I had as a kid,

06:23.310 --> 06:27.060
oh my trusty dog Wyatt passed away a couple years ago.

06:27.060 --> 06:28.108
So we'll add him,

06:28.108 --> 06:29.400
git add .

06:29.400 --> 06:33.903
git commit -m add Wyatt, rest in piece.

06:34.740 --> 06:36.840
Okay, I type git status,

06:36.840 --> 06:40.800
and now I'm ahead of origin/main by one commit, right?

06:40.800 --> 06:43.500
So if I check out origin/main,

06:43.500 --> 06:46.203
git checkout origin/ main,

06:47.460 --> 06:49.080
we see why it is gone.

06:49.080 --> 06:50.340
This is what the file looked like,

06:50.340 --> 06:51.270
this is what the repo looked

06:51.270 --> 06:54.090
like the last time we communicated with origin,

06:54.090 --> 06:55.770
which was just a couple seconds

06:55.770 --> 06:57.900
or maybe a minute ago when we pushed.

06:57.900 --> 07:00.600
So this is what it looked like if I go back

07:00.600 --> 07:02.760
which I can also do with git switch dash.

07:02.760 --> 07:06.030
We now see that we're ahead of origin/main by one commit.

07:06.030 --> 07:09.480
So now I can push that up to git push origin main,

07:09.480 --> 07:11.010
and when that finishes,

07:11.010 --> 07:13.350
we see we're now up to date with origin/main,

07:13.350 --> 07:16.140
and if I refresh, we should see a fourth commit.

07:16.140 --> 07:17.430
There it is.

07:17.430 --> 07:18.993
All right, so I'll stop there.
